# ── THE PROTOCOL EXAMPLES ARE CHECKED OUT AS THEY WERE WRITTEN ───────
#
# design/protocol/examples/ exists to prove that a developer in any
# language, on any machine, can use the node's door (design/protocol/
# THE-DOOR.md). The Linux developer is the one it is aimed at, and a
# .sh file checked out with CRLF fails on Linux with
#
#     /bin/sh: bad interpreter: No such file or directory
#
# — a message that names the interpreter and not the line ending, so the
# reader concludes the example is broken rather than the checkout. This
# repository is developed on Windows with autocrlf on; without this rule
# the guarantee holds only as long as nobody commits from an editor that
# rewrites endings.
#
# Scoped deliberately to these two patterns. A tree-wide rule would
# renormalise every file in the repository, which is a decision of its
# own and not this one. spirit/test/doorContract.js asserts these lines
# are still here.
design/protocol/examples/*.sh text eol=lf
design/protocol/examples/*.py text eol=lf
